@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# 0001: Reservation expiry is evaluated lazily against the injected clock
+
+Status: accepted (2026-10-29)
+
+## Context
+
+Holds that nobody confirms must lapse after a TTL (20 minutes by default). The package does
+no I/O and has no scheduler, and all time comes from the injected `Clock`.
+
+## Decision
+
+There's no sweeper. A reservation is *live* while it's ACTIVE and `clock.now() < expires_at`.
+Every read or use of a reservation (`get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation`,
+`release_reservation`, an idempotent `reserve` replay) first moves a lapsed ACTIVE reservation
+to EXPIRED and saves that. `held()` and therefore `available()` count only live holds, so a
+lapsed hold stops counting the moment the clock passes it, even if its stored status hasn't
+been updated yet.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A stored status can say ACTIVE for a hold that has already lapsed. Code that needs the
+  real status must go through the service (or use `expires_at`), never trust the stored
+  status alone.
+- A future database-backed repository must keep this rule. Querying "active" holds means
+  querying `status = ACTIVE AND expires_at > now`.
+- Only ACTIVE holds expire. CONFIRMED and RELEASED are final.
